@chapter 5 | Industry I: Policy, MSMEs, Start-ups and Reforms

@intro
The first industry chapter covers **industrial policy** from 1948 to 1991, **liberalisation and disinvestment**, **MSMEs** and small-scale industry committees, **start-ups, Make in India, Stand-Up India and Mudra**, infrastructure plans (**NIP, PM Gati Shakti**), CSR, the **Index of Industrial Production**, and labour and industrial relations. It contains **{pyqs} PYQs and {ones} one-liners**.
@end

@section 5.1 | Industrial Policy

@q 4:59-62

@table
Policy | Year | Key point
**Industrial Policy Resolution** | **1948** | First policy of free India; mixed economy
**Industries (Development and Regulation) Act** | **1951** | Basis of **industrial licensing** ("licence raj")
**Industrial Policy Resolution** | **1956** | "Economic constitution"; three schedules (A public, B mixed, C private); **joint sector** idea
MRTP Act | 1969 | Curbs on monopolies (replaced by the Competition Act 2002)
FERA | 1973 | Foreign exchange control (replaced by FEMA 1999)
**New Industrial Policy** | **24 July 1991** | **P.V. Narasimha Rao** and Manmohan Singh; licensing abolished except for **18** industries (now only a few: defence, hazardous chemicals, tobacco, explosives)
@end

@q 4:16-17

The order of enactment is **Minimum Wages Act (1948) → IDR Act (1951) → MRTP (1969) → FERA (1973)**. UP revises minimum wages twice a year with the dearness allowance.

@q 4:63-65

@q 4:66-67

@src1 4:1
@one U.P.P.C.S. (Spl.) (Pre) 2008
Disinvestment in Public Enterprises started from which financial year?
@ans 1991-92
@end

**Disinvestment** (selling government shares in PSUs) began in **1991-92** and is part of liberalisation; the Department is now **DIPAM**. **Air India** was privatised to the **Tata group** in January 2022. PSUs did create many jobs, so the "Reason" about employment is false.

@q 4:75-76

The **National Renewal Fund** (1992) gave a **social safety net** (retraining, compensation) for workers displaced by restructuring, including the **Voluntary Retirement Scheme**, nicknamed the **"golden handshake"**.

@section 5.2 | MSMEs and Small Industry

@q 4:21-22

@table
MSME class (from 1 July 2020; revised 2025) | Investment (plant, machinery, equipment) | Turnover
**Micro** | Up to **Rs. 1 crore** (Rs. 2.5 crore from April 2025) | Up to **Rs. 5 crore** (Rs. 10 crore)
**Small** | Up to Rs. 10 crore (Rs. 25 crore) | Up to Rs. 50 crore (Rs. 100 crore)
**Medium** | Up to Rs. 50 crore (Rs. 125 crore) | Up to Rs. 250 crore (Rs. 500 crore)
@end

The 2020 definition (announced 13 May 2020 in the **Atmanirbhar Bharat** package, effective **1 July 2020**) uses the **same composite criteria for manufacturing and services**; the effective date was not 1 June, so statements 1 and 3 are correct. Registration is on the **Udyam** portal.

@q 4:23-27

Small and cottage industries are encouraged because they **create many jobs** with little capital. Their problems are **finance, marketing and raw materials**; strikes and lockouts trouble large industry more. **Handloom** is UP's largest employer among industries.

